<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>The masses, in kg, of <m>{14}</m> bags of rice in a shop in Mombasa are listed below (not in order).</p><p style="text-align:center;">51, 46, 48, 53, 43, 29, 36, 50, 16, 17, 41, 22, 19, 15</p><p>Give exact values.</p><p>(a) Find the median.</p><div>Median <m>=</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/> kg  </div><p>(b) Find the lower quartile, <m>Q_1</m>.</p><div><m>Q_1 =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/> kg  </div><p>(c) Find the upper quartile, <m>Q_3</m>.</p><div><m>Q_3 =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans3"/> kg  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Put the data in order first. For a list of <m>n</m> values, the median is at position <m>\dfrac{n+1}{2}</m>, <m>Q_1</m> at <m>\dfrac{n+1}{4}</m> and <m>Q_3</m> at <m>\dfrac{3(n+1)}{4}</m>; <m>Q_1</m> has a quarter of the data at or below it. A position that is not a whole number lies between two values: go that fraction of the way from the lower value to the higher one.</p><p>In order: 15, 16, 17, 19, 22, 29, 36, 41, 43, 46, 48, 50, 51, 53.</p><p>(a) Position <m>\dfrac{ {15} }{2} = {7.5}</m>: halfway between <m>{36}</m> and <m>{41}</m>, so the median is <m>{38.5}</m> kg.</p><p>(b) Position <m>\dfrac{ {15} }{4} = {3.75}</m>: <m>{17} + {0.75} \times ({19} - {17}) = {18.5}</m> kg.</p><p>(c) Position <m>\dfrac{3 \times {15} }{4} = {11.25}</m>: <m>{48} + {0.25} \times ({50} - {48}) = {48.5}</m> kg.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{77}{2}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{37}{2}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{97}{2}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>